sections.text:
Common mistakes include treating the body as a simple machine with constant properties. Tissues are viscoelastic and living, so their response changes with time and load.

Other mistakes are:
forgetting units or mixing millivolts and volts;
confusing a signal with the noise that hides it;
ignoring safety, cleaning and electrical isolation;
copying a standard or regulation without checking its scope and edition;
trusting software output because it looks precise;
memorising device names without understanding the physiology they serve.

Poor documentation is also serious. Record what you did, what you assumed and what you changed. In a regulated field, an unrecorded test is hard to defend.
